  "query_preview": "..A strong area of high pressure remains over the\nregion, bringing daytime highs 5-15 degrees above normal and\nisolated showers/storms. This synoptic pattern looks to mostly\nstay in place through the forecast period, but beginning as early\nas Wednesday, the high looks to push northward, which would allow\nfor an increase in moisture across our CWA. Before diving more\ninto what's to come this week/weekend, let's discuss the heat and\nprecipitation chances for today (Tuesday). An Extreme Heat Warning\nremains in place for the lower elevations of the Grand Canyon and\nGlen/Marble Canyons, plus a Heat Advisory is in effect for much of\nnortheastern AZ. Both the U of A WRF and the SPC HREF suggest\npretty minimal convective activity this afternoon, but a few stray\nshowers/storms are possible. \n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon and Glen/Marble\nCanyons goes through Wednesday evening, but there is still\npotential for this to be extended. Hot temperatures don't look to\ngo away anytime soon, but with the projected movement of the area\nof high pressure, precipitation chances could limit how warm\ntemperatures get. Regardless, daytime highs will land 5-15 degrees\nabove normal through the end of the week and into the weekend, so\nstay hydrated and try to limit activity during the hottest part of\nthe day. Outside of the heat, shower and thunderstorm coverage\nlooks to expand westward rather than being solely focused across\nthe Rim and White Mountains for Wednesday and Thursday. Storms \nwill be capable of producing localized heavy rain, small hail, \nfrequent lightning, and gusty/erratic winds. \n\nModel guidance doesn't suggest the strong area of high pressure\nmoving out anytime soon, so continue to expect hot daytime highs\nand isolated to scattered chances for precipitation for the\nforeseeable future.",
